{
  "version": 1,
  "courseId": 1362070,
  "sourceLanguage": "en",
  "targetLanguage": "zh-TW",
  "lectures": [
    {
      "id": "25890184",
      "title": "Using Union Types",
      "sourceHash": "9116fac59301ea6347c83d7c990162af348b31affb05872d4cc3e22464e3c227",
      "cues": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "start": 2.26,
          "end": 3.864,
          "en": "Now type inference is great.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "start": 3.864,
          "end": 6.23,
          "en": "And in general, up to this point",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "start": 6.23,
          "end": 8.9,
          "en": "we already learned quite a bit",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 4,
          "start": 8.9,
          "end": 11.668,
          "en": "about TypeScript and its core types.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 5,
          "start": 11.668,
          "end": 15.512,
          "en": "But up to this point, we also always had the scenario",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 6,
          "start": 15.512,
          "end": 20.512,
          "en": "that every variable took just one type.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 7,
          "start": 20.519,
          "end": 24.127,
          "en": "So we always had just one possible type that could be stored",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 8,
          "start": 24.127,
          "end": 26.23,
          "en": "in a variable,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 9,
          "start": 26.23,
          "end": 29.49,
          "en": "just a number or just a string or just a boolean.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 10,
          "start": 29.49,
          "end": 32.122,
          "en": "And often that is what you need,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 11,
          "start": 32.122,
          "end": 36.634,
          "en": "but sometimes you want to allow multiple, different types.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 12,
          "start": 36.634,
          "end": 38.849,
          "en": "Let's say here for the course",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 13,
          "start": 38.849,
          "end": 43.76,
          "en": "we might be fine with storing a string in there",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 14,
          "start": 43.76,
          "end": 45.889,
          "en": "but maybe storing a course ID",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 15,
          "start": 45.889,
          "end": 49.02,
          "en": "as a number should also be correct.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 16,
          "start": 49.02,
          "end": 51.76,
          "en": "It really depends on what you're building.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 17,
          "start": 51.76,
          "end": 54.777,
          "en": "It's not unrealistic that we could have a variable",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 18,
          "start": 54.777,
          "end": 59.259,
          "en": "where both a string, as well as a number should be allowed",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 19,
          "start": 59.259,
          "end": 61.549,
          "en": "or that you have some variable",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 20,
          "start": 61.549,
          "end": 65.72,
          "en": "which should allow two different kinds of objects.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 21,
          "start": 65.72,
          "end": 68.725,
          "en": "And for this scenario, that you have more than one type",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 22,
          "start": 68.725,
          "end": 70.678,
          "en": "which you want to allow",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 23,
          "start": 70.678,
          "end": 73.545,
          "en": "you have a feature called union types.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 24,
          "start": 73.545,
          "end": 77.975,
          "en": "A union type is a type definition that allows more",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 25,
          "start": 77.975,
          "end": 79.322,
          "en": "than one type.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 26,
          "start": 79.322,
          "end": 83.562,
          "en": "You add it like any other type assignment by adding a colon",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 27,
          "start": 83.562,
          "end": 85.08,
          "en": "after the variable name,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 28,
          "start": 85.08,
          "end": 87.08500000000001,
          "en": "on the left side of the equal sign,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 29,
          "start": 87.08500000000001,
          "end": 88.588,
          "en": "and then your type.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 30,
          "start": 88.588,
          "end": 92.068,
          "en": "Now I mentioned that explicitly setting string",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 31,
          "start": 92.068,
          "end": 95.72,
          "en": "again here would be redundant, but it's not redundant",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 32,
          "start": 95.72,
          "end": 98.168,
          "en": "if you don't want to use the type inference here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 33,
          "start": 98.168,
          "end": 101.69,
          "en": "But if you instead want to specify such a union type,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 34,
          "start": 101.69,
          "end": 105.923,
          "en": "hence allowing multiple types, you set up such a union type",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 35,
          "start": 105.923,
          "end": 109.87899999999999,
          "en": "by adding a pipe symbol after your first type",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 36,
          "start": 109.87899999999999,
          "end": 112.735,
          "en": "and then adding another type, like number.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 37,
          "start": 112.735,
          "end": 115.85,
          "en": "And you can have as many types",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 38,
          "start": 115.85,
          "end": 118.649,
          "en": "which you want to allow here as needed,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 39,
          "start": 118.649,
          "end": 121.452,
          "en": "but here I just want to have string or number.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 40,
          "start": 121.452,
          "end": 124.26,
          "en": "And now you see that that error",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 41,
          "start": 124.26,
          "end": 127.05,
          "en": "which we had down here before is gone,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 42,
          "start": 127.05,
          "end": 130.207,
          "en": "because now we are allowing a number alternatively",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 43,
          "start": 130.207,
          "end": 132.71,
          "en": "to a string value.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 44,
          "start": 132.71,
          "end": 135.822,
          "en": "And of course you can use such a union type anywhere",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 45,
          "start": 135.822,
          "end": 138.99,
          "en": "where you have a type assignment,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 46,
          "start": 138.99,
          "end": 142.01,
          "en": "not just when you're using type inference.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 47,
          "start": 142.01,
          "end": 143.762,
          "en": "I could also use a union type up here",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 48,
          "start": 143.762,
          "end": 147.03300000000002,
          "en": "allowing username to be a string or",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 49,
          "start": 147.03300000000002,
          "end": 150.128,
          "en": "let's say an array of strings, for example",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 50,
          "start": 150.128,
          "end": 152.81,
          "en": "so that we have first and last name",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 51,
          "start": 152.81,
          "end": 154.76,
          "en": "in an array, for example.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 52,
          "start": 154.76,
          "end": 157.41,
          "en": "That's also something I could allow here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 53,
          "start": 157.41,
          "end": 161.088,
          "en": "Union types are a never key TypeScript feature",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 54,
          "start": 161.088,
          "end": 166.088,
          "en": "which allows you to define more flexible values and types.",
          "zh": ""
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "errors": []
}